title: Naval Action off Stathmore!
new-page: true
subtitle: An Imperial squadron tests the strength of the Galleon Fleet...
view-if: ivion_seen and time >= crisis_start_time + 9 and not naval_action_seen
on-arrival: naval_action_seen = 1
max-visits: 1
priority: 2
face-image: img/panzerkreuzer.jpg
tags: event

= Naval Action off Stathmore!

Wireless reports from the Stathmore coast are fragmentary but clear. An Imperial squadron crossed into contested waters this morning and shadowed the Galleon Fleet through the fog. Shortly before noon, by what the Empire will call a misunderstanding, the shadowing became gunfire. No declaration of war has followed and none will. The Empire is testing the strength of the republic's fleet.

- @observe: Trust the squadron commanders on the scene.
- @retreat: Order the ships to retreat immediately.
- @fight: Order the fleet to fight back furiously.

@observe
subtitle: The Admiralty will manage the action as it develops.
go-to: observe_weak if military_readiness < 130; observe_average if military_readiness >= 130 and military_readiness < 250; observe_strong if military_readiness >= 250

@observe_weak
on-arrival: pro_republic -= 4; coalition_dissent += 2 if marcher_in_government; commons_royalist += 3; burgher_royalist += 2; faithful_royalist += 2; loyalist_strength += 50; royalist_funds += 1; army_strength -= 10 if army_strength > 20

The action is a humiliation. Outgunned and outsailed, the fleet loses several war-galleons to capture and several more to defection, their officers taking them north under Imperial colours. The republic's weakness is now a matter of public record[? if marcher_in_government:, and our coalition partners spend the week assigning blame?]. The <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**Royalists**</span> make the obvious argument, that this is what republican stewardship of the old Imperial fleet has come to, and it carries. Their support among the Commons and the burghers rises, the Loyalists gain recruits, and the army is weakened.

@observe_average
on-arrival: commons_royalist += 2; loyalist_strength += 25

The fighting goes badly. The fleet takes more damage than it admits and falls back in good order by evening. The Admiralty calls it a successful disengagement. The <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**Royalists**</span> call it the day the Empire measured the republic and found it wanting, and their version travels further. The Loyalists gain some recruits.

@observe_strong
on-arrival: coalition_dissent -= 1 if marcher_in_government; commons_royalist -= 2; loyalist_strength -= 25

The Imperial squadron meets more resistance than it was sent to find. The two lines fight through the afternoon to a standstill, and the Imperial ships break off at dusk. No one calls it a victory, but the republic's fleet held contested waters against the Empire's, and the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**Royalists**</span>' argument about the republic's weakness loses some of its force[? if marcher_in_government:. The coalition's quarrels seem smaller for a week?].

@retreat
subtitle: Break off before the action becomes a battle.
choose-if: chancellor_party == "Marcher" or army_minister_party == "Marcher"
unavailable-subtitle: Only the chancellery or the War Ministry can issue the order.
on-arrival: commons_royalist += 3; burgher_royalist += 2; loyalist_strength += 40; royalist_funds += 1

The order goes out over the Admiralty's objections, and the fleet withdraws cleanly. No ships are lost, no officers are tested, and no battle is fought. By every sober measure this is the prudent course. It does not look prudent. The <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**Royalists**</span> print photographs of the fleet's wakes pointed south and let them speak for themselves, and their support rises. The government is at least not blamed by its own partners, since an order obeyed is not a defeat.

@fight
subtitle: The fleet is ordered to give battle in earnest.
choose-if: chancellor_party == "Marcher" or army_minister_party == "Marcher"
unavailable-subtitle: Only the chancellery or the War Ministry can issue the order.
go-to: fight_win if military_readiness >= 250; fight_disaster if military_readiness < 250

@fight_win
on-arrival: coalition_dissent -= 2 if marcher_in_government; dufour_angry -= 15; pro_republic += 3; commons_royalist -= 3; burgher_royalist -= 2; loyalist_strength -= 50; royalist_funds -= 1

The fleet turns into the Imperial line and fights the battle the Empire did not want. By nightfall the Imperial squadron is withdrawing north with two of its war-galleons under tow, and the wireless carries a report the republic has never sent before: the enemy has withdrawn. [? if president == "DuFour": President DuFour, who has never trusted a republic that could not defend itself, lets it be known that today it did, and that the order came from this government. ?]The <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**Royalists**</span>, whose whole argument rests on the republic's weakness, spend the week explaining the photographs[? if marcher_in_government:, while our coalition stands stronger than it has in a year?]. Support for the republic rises.

@fight_disaster
on-arrival: coalition_dissent += 4 if marcher_in_government; dufour_angry += 30; pro_republic -= 5; commons_royalist += 4; burgher_royalist += 3; faithful_royalist += 3; loyalist_strength += 75; royalist_funds += 2; army_strength -= 10 if army_strength > 20

The order was brave and the fleet was not ready. Ships that should have disengaged are hammered at close range, and ships that should have answered signals steam north instead. The result is a catastrophe, and unlike the weather or the Empire, the order that caused it has an author[? if marcher_in_government:. Our coalition partners say so daily?]. [? if president == "DuFour": DuFour's confidence in the government does not survive the week. ?]The <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**Royalists**</span> could not have bought a better afternoon with their whole treasury. Their support rises sharply, the Loyalists gain recruits, and the army is weakened.
